Description: A frequentist regression workflow built around inference rather
    than prediction. One engine fits gaussian, binomial, Poisson, negative
    binomial, multinomial, beta, ordinal, zero-inflated, hurdle and
    accelerated failure time models by
    approximate maximum likelihood using the Laplace approximation via 'RTMB',
    with 'lme4'-style formulas supporting crossed and nested random intercepts
    and slopes, penalised smooth terms via 'mgcv', non-constant dispersion,
    censoring, and AR(1) or CAR(1) correlation. Because one engine fits them
    all, every model shares a single object, a single set of methods, and a
    single diagnostic system that reports whether each assumption is consistent
    with the data, with a stated reason and a suggested remedy when it is not.
    Variance components may be estimated by maximum likelihood or by restricted
    maximum likelihood, and Satterthwaite or Kenward-Roger denominator degrees
    of freedom turn a mixed model's large-sample test into a finite-sample one.
    Inference extends to cluster-robust standard errors, complex survey
    designs, instrumental variables, causal mediation, estimated marginal means
    and contrasts, marginal slopes for taking an interaction with a continuous
    predictor apart, effect sizes on each family's own scale, scenario
    projection, and a-priori power by simulation. A separate entry point
    specifies factorial and repeated-measures designs by naming the
    participant, the outcome and which factors vary between or within
    participants, reporting F tests, generalized eta squared and sphericity
    corrections in the form those designs are usually reported in. The
    exploratory half -- description, outlier and multivariate anomaly
    detection, dimension reduction, clustering and profiling -- is the
    companion package 'illumex', which 'illume' attaches; imputing missing
    values and pooling across the imputations are here. Mixed models for
    nominal categorical outcomes are fitted by
    maximising the Laplace-approximated marginal likelihood rather than by
    penalised quasi-likelihood. Methods of Kristensen, Nielsen, Berg, Skaug and
    Bell (2016) <doi:10.18637/jss.v070.i05> and Halekoh and Hojsgaard (2014)
    <doi:10.18637/jss.v059.i09>.
